course: dictionary
lesson: 1038
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. The word border can mean the edge of something, or the line between two countries. The clues in the sentence tell you which meaning the writer wants. «The border of the picture was painted gold» is about an edge, because the word picture gives the clue.
explain: «Mount Everest, on the border of Nepal and China, is the highest mountain above sea level» is about a line between two countries. The place names are the clue. Always ask: which meaning fits the words around it? Then put that meaning into the sentence to check that it still makes sense.
explain: Border can also be a verb, a doing word. «Canada borders the USA» means Canada is next to the USA, with no gap between them. When you see border with a word like «the» in front, it is usually a noun. When it describes something happening, it may be a verb.
fact: A border is the edge of something, such as a picture frame or a cloth.
fact: A border is the line between two countries, states or places.
fact: «To border» means to be next to something, as in «Canada borders the USA».
fact: «Countries also have borders at sea, with territorial waters reaching 12 nautical miles from the coast.» A nautical mile is a sea mile, a bit under 2 kilometres.
fact: «Real borders around 1337 were often blurry zones», not the sharp lines we draw on maps today.
exercise: «The border of the page was decorated with stars.» What does border mean here? | *The edge of something | To be next to something | A long strip of flowers in a garden | The line between two countries
exercise: «Mount Everest, on the border of Nepal and China, is the highest mountain above sea level.» What kind of border is this? | *A line between two countries | The edge of a picture frame | A rope to keep people back | A strip of flowers along a path
exercise: «Canada borders the USA.» What does borders mean? | *Is next to | Crosses over with a passport | Forgets | Paints a frame around
test: «The garden border was full of red and yellow tulips.» What is a border here? | The height of a mountain | To be next to something | A line between two countries | *A long strip of flowers along an edge
test: Which sentence uses border as a verb? | The border of the map was torn | *The farm borders a forest | Our house has a blue border | We cross the border on Sunday
exam: «The border between the two villages was a river, but people crossed it every day.» What does border mean in this sentence? | A strip of flowers in a garden | *The line between two places | To be next to something, as a verb | The edge of a picture
